# Design Philosophy

* Keep It Simple
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.code_design.how_to_guide.md:85 '### Keep It Simple'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.code_design.how_to_guide.md:103 '#### Designing Software Systems Is Tricky'

- @Definition@: **keep it simple** (KISS) means to pick the simplest design that
  solves the problem
  - Some problems are complex, but a complex system still splits into simple
    pieces
  - A simple design is easier to understand, modify, and debug

// TODO(gp): Check the reference for the Jeff Dean talk: the material cites
// slides of a Stanford talk, with no title and no year
- @Fact@: a design balances six goals, in this order _(Dean, 2007)_
  - Simplicity first, then scalability, performance, reliability, and
    generality
  - Features come last

// TODO(gp): Check the plugin system example: it is an illustration, not taken
// from a source
- @Example@: asked for a function that makes a short code, an agent may add a
  plugin system for code generators that nobody asked for
  - Each extra concept is one more thing a reviewer must read and keep

- @Remark@: the principles of good design are covered in detail in Lesson 14.01

* Separate What Changes From What Stays the Same
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:2159 '#### Always Separate What Changes From What Stays the Same'

- @Problem@: copy-paste-modify hides the one line that differs between two
  blocks
  - A reader must diff the copies by eye
  - A fix lands in one copy and not in the others

- @Solution@: put the common part in a function, and pass what changes as
  parameters
  - E.g., two test blocks that differ only in a list of stop words become two
    calls of one `_helper(texts, categories, expected)`

- @Intuition@: the same rule works at the scale of the architecture
  - Put the part that is likely to change behind a stable interface
  - The rest of the code depends on the interface, not on that part

// TODO(gp): Check the storage interface example: shortlink is not written yet,
// so the function name is illustrative
- @Example@: the storage engine of `shortlink` may change from SQLite to
  Postgres
  - The service layer calls `storage.get_link(code)`, and never writes SQL
  - Changing the engine then touches one module

* Pure Functions by Default
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.code_design.how_to_guide.md:182 '### Prefer Pure Functions by Default'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.code_design.how_to_guide.md:157 '### Avoid Modifying the Function Input'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:300 '## `ARCH2.4`: Do Not Modify Inputs'

- @Definition@: a **pure function** has two properties
  - The same arguments always give the same return value
  - Its evaluation has no side effects: no global state, no I/O, no change to
    its inputs

- @Pros@
  - Easy to understand, and easy to test with plain inputs
  - Safe to refactor, move, and chain
  - Gives the same result when a notebook runs its cells out of order

- @Techniques@: do not modify an input, return a new object (`ARCH2.4`)
  - An in-place function is allowed only when a copy costs too much, e.g., a
    huge `pd.DataFrame`
  - Name it `<name>_inplace`, and say so in its docstring

- @Remark@: the rule is a default, not a dogma
  - I/O must happen somewhere: keep it in a thin layer at the edge, as the next
    section shows

* Invariants as Written Rules
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.code_design.how_to_guide.md:203 '## Invariants'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:43 '## Seeded Flaws in `v0`'

- @Definition@: an **invariant** is a property that every part of the system
  keeps true, whoever changes the code

- @Example@: a trading system and its clock
  - Each object asks `get_wall_clock_time()` for the current time
  - No object passes `wall_clock_timestamp` to another one
  - _Why_: an object that is told the time cannot check it, so a wrong time
    lets future data leak into a past decision

- @Example@: two invariants of `shortlink`
  - Every short code maps to exactly one URL
  - Every time is compared in UTC
  - `v0` breaks both: a race gives two links one code, and `expires_at` is
    compared in local time

- @Key idea@: an invariant written in the design doc is a rule that an agent
  can follow and a test can check
  - An invariant that lives in a head is a bug that waits for the next change

# Layers, Data Flow, and Pipelines

* Organize Code in Layers
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:5 '## `ARCH1.1`: Organize Code in Layers'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: a **layer** is a group of code with one job (`ARCH1.1`)
  - _Script_: parse the CLI, in `_parse()`
  - _Orchestration_: call the layers in order, in `_main()`
  - _Business logic_: transform data, with no I/O
  - _Infrastructure_: files, DBs, and external APIs
  - _Utilities_: generic helpers

- @Techniques@: dependencies point one way
  - Logic and infrastructure call only utilities
  - Orchestration passes the data between them

// TODO(gp): Check the mapping of the shortlink modules onto the layers: the
// module `codes.py` is illustrative, since shortlink is not written yet
- @Example@: in `shortlink`, `service.py` orchestrates, `codes.py` holds the
  logic, and `storage.py` talks to SQLite
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

