:::

* Explicit Data Flow
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:150 '## `ARCH2.1`: Pass Data Through Arguments and Return Values'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:193 '## `ARCH2.2`: Bundle Parameters Into a Config Dataclass'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:248 '## `ARCH2.3`: Return and Pass Dataclasses, Not Tuples'

- @Problem@: hidden data dependencies
  - `stage2()` reads a global that `stage1()` sets, so the order of the calls
    is a contract that no signature shows
  - `analyze()` returns `(mean, std, count, median)`, so each caller must
    remember the order

- @Solution@: make every data dependency visible in a signature
  - Pass data through arguments and return values, with type hints
    (`ARCH2.1`)
  - Bundle more than 3 options that tune a function into a config dataclass
    (`ARCH2.2`)
  - Return a dataclass for 3 or more values, or for values with different
    meanings (`ARCH2.3`)

- @Remark@: a config states _what_ to do, and the code says _how_
  - Never store code or an expression in a config value, e.g.,
    `"if value > 0 then log(value) else 0"`

- @Key idea@: an agent that reads only a signature knows what a function needs
  and what it returns

* Validate at the Boundary
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:726 '## `ARCH4.1`: Validate at the Boundary'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:43 '## Seeded Flaws in `v0`'

- @Problem@: a check in every layer, or a check deep in the processing
  - Repeated checks drift apart
  - A bad option fails after minutes of work, far from its cause

- @Solution@: validate the user input and the option combinations once, at the
  entry, and then trust them (`ARCH4.1`)
  - In `_main()`, right after parsing, with `hdbg.dassert_*` and a message
  - Keep a cheap `dassert` inside only for an invariant whose break gives a
    wrong result with no error

- @Example@: the open redirect of `shortlink` `v0`
  - `POST /links` accepts any URL scheme, e.g., `javascript:`
  - The fix is one check in the API layer: only `http` and `https`
  - The service and storage layers do not repeat it

- @Key idea@: the boundary is where untrusted data enters, so it is the one
  place that must doubt it

* Split Sequential Processing Into Stages
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:331 '## `ARCH3.1`: Split Sequential Processing Into Stages'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:379 '## `ARCH3.2`: Select Stages with Hselect_action'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:433 '## `ARCH3.3`: Pass Data Between Selectable Stages Through Files'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:2216 '#### Organize Scripts as Pipelines'

- @Definition@: a **pipeline** splits 3 or more sequential steps into stages
  (`ARCH3.1`), and each stage
  - Has one job
  - Takes its input as an argument and returns its output
  - Is idempotent: running it twice gives the same result
  - Depends on other stages only through its input

- @Techniques@: let the user run a subset of the stages with `hselect_action`
  - `--action`, `--skip_action`, and `--all_actions`
  - A selectable stage reads its input from a file and writes its output to a
    file, since the stage before it may not have run

- @Pros@
  - Test each stage alone
  - Rerun only the expensive stage
  - Add, remove, or reorder stages

// TODO(gp): Check the analytics stages: shortlink is not written yet, so the
// stage names are illustrative
- @Example@: click analytics in `shortlink`: `collect` $\to$ `aggregate` $\to$
  `report`, each stage a function of the output of the stage before

* When to Split a Pipeline Into Executables
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:455 '## `ARCH3.4`: Split a Pipeline Into Executables'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:479 '## `ARCH3.5`: Sequence Executables with a Driver Script'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:534 '## `ARCH3.6`: Number the Output Files of Each Executable'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: split a pipeline into one executable per stage (`ARCH3.4`)
  when the stages differ in
  - Dependencies, e.g., a GPU versus only `pandas`
  - Runtime, e.g., hours versus seconds
  - Schedule, e.g., daily versus weekly

- @Techniques@: executables talk only through files
  - Paths come from CLI arguments
  - Files are named `NN-<stage>.<what>.<ext>`
  - A driver `run_<flow>.sh` runs them in order

- @Remark@: otherwise keep one script with `hselect_action`, which is simpler
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

