# Package Structure

* What a Package Is For
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md:19 '## Goals of packages'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md:49 '### Rules for imports'

- @Definition@: a **package** is a dir with an `__init__.py` that exports one
  interface for a group of related modules

- @Goal@: what a package buys
  - Clients import one name, not the file that holds the code
  - The files inside can move with no change to the clients
  - Related units sit together, and import loops become preventable

- @Example@: the same class, through the package or through its file
  \begingroup \scriptsize
  ```python
  # Through the package: the client does not see the file.
  import dataflow.core as dtfcore
  dtfcore.ArmaGenerator(...)
  # Through the file: the client breaks when the class moves.
  import dataflow_amp.system.source_nodes as dtfsysonod
  dtfsysonod.ArmaGenerator(...)
  ```
  \endgroup

- @Remark@: the import rules and the cycle detector are covered in detail in
  Lesson 07.01

* Anatomy of a Package
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md:162 '## Anatomy of a package'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md:92 '### How to import code from unit tests'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md:120 '#### Common unit test code'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.make_function_private/SKILL.md:31 '# Private Functions'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Components@
  - `__init__.py`: the public names, and the import alias in its docstring
  - One module per job, named after it (`ARCH1.4`)
  - `<name>_example.py`: example objects for the tests
  - `test/`: the unit tests, and the shared `<name>_test_case.py`

- @Techniques@: keep the public surface small
  - A function that no other file calls gets a `_` prefix
  - `coding.make_function_private` renames these functions and fixes the
    callers

- @Remark@: tests are leaves of the import graph
  - A test never imports another test
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

// TODO(gp): Check the layout of the shortlink package: it is illustrative,
// since shortlink is not written yet
- @Example@: the files of `shortlink/`
  ```text
  __init__.py
  api.py
  service.py
  codes.py
  storage.py
  types.py
  shortlink_example.py
  test/test_codes.py
  test/test_service.py
  ```
::::
:::

* A Package Hierarchy
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md:130 '## Package/lib hierarchy and cycle prevention'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=62%}
- @Definition@: a **package hierarchy** orders the packages, and each package
  imports only the packages below it
  - `helpers`: only external libraries
  - `core`: only `helpers`
  - `dataflow`: only `core` and `helpers`

- @Techniques@: the same order holds inside one package
  - E.g., `hgit` may import `hsystem`, and `hsystem` may import `hdbg`, never
    the reverse

- @Intuition@: a tool states the order as source nodes, or as forbidden edges,
  of the import graph

- @Remark@: never break a loop with an import inside a function
  - Split the file instead
::::
:::: {.column width=38%}

```graphviz[width=65%]
digraph Hierarchy {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.40;
    ranksep=0.40;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Courier", fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.07",
          height=0.36, width=1.3];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // package : blue | external : violet | forbidden import : coral

    Dataflow [label="dataflow",
              fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Core     [label="core",
              fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Helpers  [label="helpers",
              fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Ext      [label="external libs", fontname="Helvetica",
              fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];

    Dataflow -> Core;
    Dataflow -> Helpers;
    Core -> Helpers;
    Helpers -> Ext;
    Helpers -> Core [color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A", style=dashed,
                     label="  Forbidden  "];
}
```
label=fig:package-hierarchy
caption=Allowed imports point down the package hierarchy (blue), and an upward edge (coral) is forbidden

::::
:::

* Checking the Hierarchy in CI
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:92 '## `ARCH1.3`: Enforce Dependency Direction'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.import_check.reference.md:19 '# show_imports'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Code_Visualization.md:35 '## Research Topics'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Lesson12.01_Planning_Software.smd:605 '* Architecture Rules During Planning'

- @Problem@: a rule that no check enforces holds only until the next agent run

// TODO(gp): Check the test: `get_import_edges()` is a hypothetical helper, and
// the layer map of shortlink is illustrative
- @Solution@: a unit test maps each module to a layer, and fails on an import
  that does not point down
  \begingroup \scriptsize
  ```python
  _LAYER = {"api": 3, "service": 2, "codes": 1, "storage": 1, "types": 0}

  def test_imports_point_down() -> None:
      for module, imported in get_import_edges("shortlink"):
          assert _LAYER[imported] < _LAYER[module], (module, imported)
  ```
  \endgroup

// TODO(gp): Check that `import-linter` checks layer contracts: it is recalled
// from memory, and the material says only that `grimp` is used by it
- @Techniques@: tools that draw or check the graph
  - `show_imports --dir` draws the imports between dirs, and `--show_cycles`
    only the cycles
  - `grimp` builds the import graph of a package, and `import-linter` checks
    layer contracts on that graph

- @Example@: in the plan of `shortlink`, `PR_T1` adds this test, so storage
  cannot import the service from the first PR on

# Repo Structure

* Monorepo Versus Multi-Repo
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part2.runnable_directory.tex:74 '\subsection{Problem}'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:318 '* Single vs Multiple Repo'

- @Problem@: teams want the modularity of many repos and the consistent
  environment of one repo, and the classic answer gives up one of the two

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Approach", "Pros", "Cons"),
  rows: (
    ("Monorepo",
     "One commit history, no version skew, shared tools and standards",
     "Slow CI, build tools like Bazel, weak boundaries, hard permissions"),
    ("Multi-repo",
     "Independent release cycles, small checkouts, clear ownership",
     "Version conflicts between repos, costly change propagation"),
  ),
  caption: "The classic trade-off between one repo and many",
  col-widths: (0.7fr, 1.4fr, 1.6fr),
)
```

// TODO(gp): Check the reference for the Google monorepo: the material gives
// only the ACM link https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2854146
- @Example@: Google, Meta, Microsoft, and Uber keep large codebases in one repo
  _(Potvin and Levenberg, 2016)_

- @Key idea@: the real question is not _"one repo or many?"_, but _"what is the
  smallest unit that builds, tests, and ships on its own?"_

* What Each Choice Costs an Agent
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:476 '* Automating Cross-Repo Renames and Reference Updates'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:348 '* Runnable Directories: A Hybrid Architecture'

// TODO(gp): Check the trade-offs for an agent: they are the analysis of the
// author, not the result of a measured study
- @Comparison@: the same trade-off, seen by an agent
  - _Monorepo_: one `grep` finds every caller, and a change is one atomic PR,
    but the context is huge
  - _Multi-repo_: the context is small, but a rename must reach every repo
    that depends on the code

- @Solution@: skills that carry one move across repos
  - `git.move` moves a file and rewrites the references in its repo
  - `git.update_references` updates the references in the other repos
  - `coding.rename` does the same for a function or a variable

- @Key idea@: the propagation cost that makes multi-repo expensive for a human
  is the mechanical work that an agent automates cheaply

- @Remark@: a boundary that matches the unit of work also bounds the context of
  the agent: prompt, container, and test scope coincide

* Runnable Directories: A Hybrid
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part2.runnable_directory.tex:151 '\subsection{Solution: Runnable directories}'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part2.runnable_directory.tex:295 '\subsubsection{Runnable directory}'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md:132 '## Runnable dir'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: a **runnable directory** is a dir with its own `devops` dir,
  so it builds its own container, and runs, tests, and ships as a unit
  - A repo is a runnable dir at the top
  - A runnable dir can hold other ones, or include one as a submodule

- @Pros@: modularity and consistency at once
  - A dir adds a package to its own container only
  - All dirs share one toolchain, and the code stays as reachable as in a
    monorepo

- @Cons@: a team must learn containers, and they cost memory on a laptop

- @Example@: `//cmamp/infra` is a runnable dir in the repo `//cmamp`
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz[width=85%]
digraph Runnable {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.40;
    rankdir=TB;
    compound=true;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.06",
          height=0.32];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // devops of a runnable dir : sage green | separate repo : violet

    subgraph cluster_a {
        label = "Runnable repo A"; labelloc = "t";
        fontname = "Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize = 11; fontcolor = "#3A4A5C";
        style = "rounded,filled"; fillcolor = "#F7F9FB"; color = "#C7D0DA";
        penwidth = 1.0; margin = 14;

        DevA [label="devops A",
              fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];

        subgraph cluster_b {
            label = "Runnable dir B"; labelloc = "t";
            fontname = "Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize = 10; fontcolor = "#3A4A5C";
            style = "rounded,filled"; fillcolor = "#F7F9FB"; color = "#C7D0DA";
            penwidth = 1.0; margin = 10;
            DevB [label="devops B",
                  fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
        }

        subgraph cluster_c1 {
            label = "Submodule C"; labelloc = "t";
            fontname = "Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize = 10; fontcolor = "#3A4A5C";
            style = "rounded,filled"; fillcolor = "#F7F9FB"; color = "#C7D0DA";
            penwidth = 1.0; margin = 10;
            DevC1 [label="devops C",
                   fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
        }
    }

    RepoC [label="Repo C",
           fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];

    RepoC -> DevC1 [lhead=cluster_c1, style=dashed, label="  Submodule  "];
}
```
label=fig:runnable-dirs
caption=A runnable repo holds a runnable subdir and a submodule; each has its own devops (green), and the submodule points to a separate repo (violet)

::::
:::

* The `//helpers` Submodule and Shared Files
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part2.runnable_directory.tex:480 '\subsubsection{Submodule of ``helpers''}'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md:223 '## Managing common files'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md:248 '### Automated linking for standard configuration files'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part2.runnable_directory.tex:438 '\subsubsection{Thin environment}'

- @Definition@: every repo includes `//helpers` as a Git submodule, with the
  shared utilities and the dev toolchain
  - Thin environment, linter, Docker and `invoke` workflows, and Git hooks

- @Problem@: files that must sit in each repo but are the same everywhere drift
  apart when they are copied
  - E.g., `pyproject.toml`, `pytest.ini`, `.pre-commit-config.yaml`,
    `CLAUDE.md`, and `.claude/`

- @Solution@: keep one copy in `//helpers`, and link to it
  - `create_all_helpers_links.py` creates the read-only symlinks
  - To customize a file, first replace its link with a copy

- @Definition@: the **thin environment** is a small Python environment with
  only what starts the dev containers, e.g., Docker and `invoke`
  - One thin environment serves all the runnable dirs

- @Key idea@: one change in `//helpers` reaches every repo, so the agent
  instructions, the lint rules, and the hooks never drift apart

* Imports and Tests Across Runnable Dirs
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md:344 '## Module imports'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md:390 '### Recursive pytest'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part2.runnable_directory.tex:533 '\subsubsection{Executing tests}'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Techniques@: imports respect the structure of the repo
  - A runnable dir imports from the root of its parent repo, e.g.,
    `//cmamp/optimizer` imports from `//cmamp`
  - Only a repo that can be a submodule, e.g., `//helpers`, imports from its
    own root

- @Algorithm@: recursive tests
  1. `main_pytest.py` visits each runnable dir
  2. It runs the tests of the dir in its own container
  3. One command runs the whole suite, even with conflicting packages

- @Remark@: a runnable dir can move in the tree with no change to its config
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz[width=80%]
digraph Pytest {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.20;
    ranksep=0.40;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.06",
          height=0.32];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7];

    // driver : teal | runnable dir in its container : blue

    Main [label="main_pytest.py",  fontname="Courier",
          fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    D1   [label=<<b>Container 1</b><br/>pytest on dir 1>,
          fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    D2   [label=<<b>Container 2</b><br/>pytest on dir 2>,
          fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Dn   [label=<<b>Container n</b><br/>pytest on dir n>,
          fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Main -> D1;
    Main -> D2;
    Main -> Dn;
}
```
label=fig:recursive-pytest
caption=The driver (teal) runs the tests of each runnable dir in its own container (blue)

::::
:::

# Architecture Diagrams

* Diagrams That Can Be Checked
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:113 '### Mapping C4 and Code Structure'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:244 '#### Our Conventions'

- @Remark@: the C4 model and its PlantUML conventions are covered in detail in
  Lesson 08.01

- @Problem@: a diagram drawn once from memory shows the architecture that the
  team intended, not the one in the code

- @Solution@: make every element of the diagram point at the code
  - Each box is a path: a top-level dir for C2, a second-level dir or a module
    for C3
  - Each box has exactly the name of its dir, file, or class, even if longer
  - Each arrow is an import, drawn in the direction of the dependency

- @Pros@: the check becomes mechanical
  - A box with no path is a component that does not exist
  - An import with no arrow is a dependency that nobody designed

- @Key idea@: an architecture diagram is a claim about the code, and a claim
  that a script can test

* A C3 View of `shortlink`
// From: .claude/skills/C4.rules.md:46 '## Start Every Diagram with the Same Header'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:22 '## What It Is'

// TODO(gp): Check the components of shortlink: the module names are
// illustrative, since shortlink is not written yet
- @Example@: the intended components of `shortlink` after this lesson
  - One box per module, every arrow points down a layer, and `types.py` is a
    leaf
  - The source is 15 lines of text in `02-design.architecture.md`, and the
    import test checks the same arrows

```plantuml[width=85%]
!pragma layout smetana
!include <C4/C4_Component>
LAYOUT_LEFT_RIGHT()
title shortlink: Components
Container_Boundary(app, "shortlink") {
  Component(api, "api.py", "", "Parses requests")
  Component(service, "service.py", "", "Orders the steps")
  Component(codes, "codes.py", "", "Makes short codes")
  Component(storage, "storage.py", "", "Reads and writes links")
  Component(types, "types.py", "", "Shared dataclasses")
}
ContainerDb(db, "links.db", "SQLite", "Links and clicks")
Rel(api, service, "Calls")
Rel(service, codes, "Calls")
Rel(service, storage, "Calls")
Rel(codes, types, "Imports")
Rel(storage, types, "Imports")
Rel(storage, db, "Reads and writes", "SQL")
```
label=fig:shortlink-c3
caption=C3 view of shortlink: five modules in one container, every arrow pointing down a layer, and a leaf module of shared types

* Intended Versus Actual Architecture
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.import_check.reference.md:71 '## Visualize dependencies at a directory level'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:181 '### Generating Class Diagram'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:158 '### Use Classes!'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
// TODO(gp): Check the term "architecture drift" and add a reference, e.g., to
// the literature on architecture erosion: the term is not in the material
- @Definition@: **architecture drift** is the gap between the intended diagram
  and the imports in the code

- @Techniques@: generate the actual picture from the code
  - `show_imports --dir` draws the import graph
  - `create_class_diagram.sh` draws the classes

- @Algorithm@: compare the arrows of the diagram with the edges of the graph
  1. An edge with no arrow is a violation
  2. An arrow with no edge is a stale diagram

- @Remark@: an agent diffs the two texts well, and a human decides whether to
  fix the code or the diagram
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz[width=75%]
digraph Drift {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.35;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Courier", fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.06",
          height=0.32];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // module : blue | edge only in code : red | arrow only in diagram : dotted

    Api     [label="api.py",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Service [label="service.py",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Codes   [label="codes.py",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Storage [label="storage.py",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Types   [label="types.py",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    { rank=same; Codes; Storage; }

    Api -> Service;
    Service -> Codes;
    Service -> Storage;
    Storage -> Types;
    Codes -> Types [style=dotted, color="#8C8C8C", label="  Stale  "];
    Storage -> Service [color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6,
                        label="  Violation  "];
}
```
label=fig:drift
caption=Intended and actual imports agree (gray), except one import found only in the code (red) and one arrow found only in the diagram (dotted)

::::
:::

# Architecture Decisions

* Which Choices Deserve a Record
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:293 '* Architecture Decision Records'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:375 '## Record Every Non-Obvious Choice'

- @Remark@: the format of a decision record is covered in detail in Lesson
  08.01

- @Problem@: rules and manifests say what to do today, not why an earlier
  option lost
  - An agent asked to _"simplify"_ reopens a settled trade-off every time

// TODO(gp): Check the three criteria: they are a synthesis of the author, and
// the material says only "record every non-obvious choice"
- @Definition@: an architecture choice needs a record when it is
  - Hard to reverse, e.g., a storage engine, a repo split, a public interface
  - Across components: it changes how two or more of them talk
  - A departure from a rule: the record cites the tag, e.g., `ARCH1.3`

- @Techniques@: link the record from the rule or the design doc that it
  explains
  - The agent then finds the reason when it loads the rule

- @Key idea@: a rule file says what to do today, and a decision record says why
  this rule beat the others

* A Decision Record: SQLite Versus Postgres
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:72 '## Chapter Fit'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Lesson12.01_Planning_Software.smd:732 '* Decisions Made While Planning'

// TODO(gp): The decision record is illustrative: the shortlink decision log
// does not exist yet. Sync the id and the content with `03-decisions.md` once
// it is written
- @Example@: the decision of this lesson, shortened
  \begingroup \scriptsize
  ```text
  D10 (Keep SQLite Behind a Storage Interface)
  - Status: accepted
  - Context: v1 runs two processes, and both write clicks and counters
  - Decision: keep SQLite in WAL mode for v1
    - Only storage.py knows the engine
  - Alternatives
    - Postgres now: many writers, but one more service to run, back up,
      and start in every test
    - SQLite and no interface: simplest, but a switch hits every layer
  - Consequences
    - The tests stay hermetic: one file, no server
    - Reopen when the writes exceed one SQLite writer: then the switch
      changes storage.py only
  ```
  \endgroup

- @Remark@: the record names the trigger that reverses it, so the agent knows
  when not to reopen it

* Service Boundaries and Scalability
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:455 '## `ARCH3.4`: Split a Pipeline Into Executables'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:2306 '#### Think About Scalability'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:1 '# Running Example: `shortlink`'

- @Question@: should the click analytics of `shortlink` run in the web process,
  or as a separate service?

// TODO(gp): Check the service boundary criteria: they extend ARCH3.4 and are a
// synthesis of the author; the microservices reference is recalled from memory
- @Comparison@:
  - _One process_: one deploy, one log, function calls, one transaction
  - _Separate service_: scales and fails apart from the redirects, but adds a
    network call, a queue, and a second deploy _(Lewis and Fowler, 2014)_

- @Definition@: split a service along the same lines as an executable, when
  its parts differ in
  - Load: redirects are many and fast, reports are rare and slow
  - Failure: a crash of the reports must not stop the redirects
  - Schedule or owner: a nightly job, or another team

- @Techniques@: measure on data of the real size before splitting, e.g.,
  one-row SQL inserts do not scale to 100 GB

- @Key idea@: start with one process and clean layers, since a clean layer
  boundary is the cheapest place to cut a service later

* What AI Adds to an Architecture Decision
// From: research/ideas/draft.AI_Architecture_Decision_Support.md:8 '## Core Idea'
// From: research/ideas/draft.AI_Architecture_Decision_Support.md:49 '## Research Topics'
// From: research/ideas/draft.AI_Technical_Debt_Management.md:8 '## Core Idea'

- @Techniques@: what an agent does well today
  - Extract the structure of a codebase: modules, coupling, and cycles
  - Draft a decision record from a discussion, with alternatives and
    consequences
  - List the call sites that a decision would change

// TODO(gp): Check the references of the two research ideas: several entries
// look unreliable (years, numbers), so only Kruchten et al. and Ford et al.
// are cited here, and both are recalled from memory
- @Challenges@: what is still research
  - Score the options on quality attributes: performance, scalability,
    maintainability, and security
  - Flag when a refactoring is due, from trends in coupling and complexity
  - Price architecture violations as technical debt _(Kruchten et al., 2019)_

- @Definition@: an **architecture fitness function** is an automated check of
  how well the code keeps an architectural goal _(Ford et al., 2017)_
  - The import test of `shortlink` is one

- @Limitations@: a model knows the code, not the constraints around it
  - Budget, team skills, and what the business will need next

- @Key idea@: the agent drafts the options and checks them, and a human owns
  the decision

# Modularizing Code With AI

* Factoring Common Code
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:452 '* Automating Code Modularization with AI'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.factor_common_code/SKILL.md:27 '## Guidelines'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.factor_common_code/SKILL.md:37 '## Output Format'

- @Problem@: generated code duplicates helper logic across files, since each
  generation starts from the local context only

- @Solution@: the skill `coding.factor_common_code`
  1. Read the given files
  2. Find exact and near-duplicate blocks
  3. Propose a function: name, parameters, and return value
  4. Report the locations as `file.py: l1-l2`, plus a vim quickfix file
  5. Refactor only after a human accepts, with no change in behavior

- @Techniques@: guidelines that keep the refactoring honest
  - Skip extractions of fewer than 2-3 meaningful lines
  - Prefer logic with one clear job that will change in one place
  - `--dry_run` reports and changes nothing

- @Key idea@: duplication is easy to detect and easy to overdo, so the tool
  proposes and a human decides

* Encoding the Architecture as Rules
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.rules.md:3 '# ARCH1: Layers and Dependencies'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.write_design/SKILL.md:32 '## Split Into Components'
// From: .claude/skills/skill.add_architecture/SKILL.md:6 '# Goal'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: `architecture.rules.md` holds the architecture as tagged
  rules
  - `ARCH1` layers, `ARCH2` data flow, `ARCH3` pipelines, `ARCH4` validation

- @Techniques@: the same tags flow through every step
  - `architecture.write_design` uses them to split a PRD into components
  - The agent loads them before it organizes code
  - A reviewer cites one, e.g., _"split this module: `ARCH1.4`"_
  - `skill.add_architecture` adds a new rule

- @Key idea@: a tagged rule is a shared vocabulary for humans, agents, and
  checks
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz[width=75%]
digraph RuleLoop {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.32;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.06",
          height=0.34, width=1.9];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // rules : violet | design : teal | code : blue | checks : sage green

    Rules  [label="architecture.rules.md", fontname="Courier",
            fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];
    Design [label="Design docs",
            fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Code   [label="Code by the agent",
            fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Check  [label="Import test, review",
            fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];

    Rules -> Design [label="  Splits into\n  components  "];
    Design -> Code;
    Code -> Check;
    Check -> Rules [style=dashed, constraint=false,
                    label="  New rule  "];
}
```
label=fig:rule-loop
caption=The rules (violet) shape the design (teal) and the code (blue); the checks (green) find new cases that become new rules

::::
:::

* Modularizing `shortlink` With an Agent
// From:

// TODO(gp): The workflow is an illustration that combines the tools of this
// lesson: it was not run on shortlink
- @Algorithm@: the task of this lesson, as a sequence of small PRs
  1. _Map_: `show_imports --dir shortlink --show_cycles` finds the cycle
  2. _Intend_: write the C3 diagram and the layer of each module in the design
     doc
  3. _Guard_: add the import test, which fails on `v0`
  4. _Fix_: move `Link` to `types.py`, and the test passes
  5. _Shrink_: run `coding.make_function_private` on each module
  6. _Factor_: run `coding.factor_common_code --dry_run`, and accept what is
     worth it
  7. _Record_: write `D10`, SQLite behind a storage interface

- @Remark@: the steps split between the agent and the human
  - Steps 1, 3, 4, and 5 are mechanical, and the agent runs them alone
  - Steps 2, 6, and 7 are choices that a human approves

- @Key idea@: the test comes before the fix, so the agent knows when it is done
  and the cycle cannot come back

* Summary

- @Key idea@: an agent edits from local context, so the architecture must live
  in rules, tests, diagrams, and records that a machine reads
- Keep it simple, separate what changes from what stays the same, and default
  to pure functions and written invariants
- Layers have one job each, imports point down, data flows through signatures,
  and the input is checked once at the boundary
- A pipeline splits into stages, and into executables when the stages differ
  in dependencies, runtime, or schedule
- A package hides its files behind `__init__.py`, and a unit test enforces the
  package hierarchy
- Runnable dirs give the modularity of many repos with the one environment of a
  monorepo, through `//helpers` and shared links
- A diagram whose boxes are paths can be checked against the import graph of
  the code
- A decision record says why an option won and when to reopen it: the agent
  drafts, and the human decides

